    Build the portfolio page
    
    `/portfolio` shows past work grouped by category, and `/portfolio/[slug]` gives
    each piece a page with its yarn type, colour, and — where the pattern is not
    the maker's own — a designer credit.
    
    A typed content module rather than a database table. docs/backend.md section 6
    lists the portfolio as needing no server, and that still holds: this is
    read-only content that never changes per request, so both routes prerender and
    the build needs no database. The portfolio_items and portfolio_images tables
    stay in the schema for whenever admin management arrives; moving to them is the
    same one-file swap the shop demonstrated.
    
    Deliberately not built on the shop's components. A portfolio piece has no
    price, no stock and no cart — it is past work, and the route onward is a
    commission. Sharing ProductCard would have meant threading "is this for sale?"
    through both pages, and the two answer different questions. A test asserts no
    portfolio item carries priceCents or stock, so the distinction cannot erode
    quietly.
    
    Categories are data, not code. Clothes and Plushies have items; Crochet,
    Engineering and Jewelry are declared, empty, and do not render — so launching
    one is a content change. The grouping helper drops empty categories, and tests
    cover both that it loses no items and that it duplicates none.
    
    The designer credit renders only when there is one. An empty label or an
    "unknown" would imply a missing attribution where the design is the maker's
    own, so the placeholder data deliberately contains both cases and the tests
    assert both renderings.
    
